-- examples/checks/agg-widen-safety-check.rofl — agg_widen_safety: the rules
-- of agg-widen-safety-1-ok.rofl are accepted and evaluate: the loop head
-- widened within (-inf,inf) — [0,0], then its body's [1,1] and the negated
-- body's [-1,-1] take both ends past it — so every point that reads it a
-- shrug inherited from it, and entry exactly [0,0]; every
-- agg-widen-safety-N-refused-*.rofl is refused, each for the reason its first
-- line names (the harness holds the text), so none of their rules reaches
-- this world: an interval function on a cycle no widening bounds (a hull
-- through itself, and through two hulls neither declaring one), a widening
-- declared for another lattice than a hull, two widenings of one relation,
-- an interval function's value carried into a plain relation, one computed
-- from an order lattice's value, ivmul by the value, and one between two
-- cells of one hull that declares no widening (a cycle of that relation,
-- judged as every safety rule judges, over relations).
awsf_accepted(Rel)  :- concludes[$kernel](_, Rel), awsf_refused(Rel).
awsf_refused(awsr_self).
awsf_refused(awsr_pair).
awsf_refused(awsr_mate).
awsf_refused(awsr_union).
awsf_refused(awsr_min).
awsf_refused(awsr_two).
awsf_refused(awsr_plainv).
awsf_refused(awsr_order).
awsf_refused(awsr_mul).
awsf_refused(awsr_keys).
awsf_unwidened()    :- not shrug[$kernel]($lattice(aws_head, main, 0, $cons(h, $nil)), widened, within(iv(ninf, inf))).
awsf_lost()         :- not aws_pt(entry, iv(0, 0)).
awsf_lost()         :- not aws_plain(iv(0, 0)).
alarm(awsf_accepted).
alarm(awsf_unwidened).
alarm(awsf_lost).
-- expect-row: aws_out[main](entry,iv(ninf,0))
-- expect-row: shrug[$kernel](aws_derived(twice,$unknown_value),inherited,
-- expect-no-row: aws_pt[main](body,
